    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test run, 0 failures \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class; the other two files are the base/support classes).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec asked to *define* the membership-level-4 rule (tenure > 365 days) such that a newly created owner \u2014 with zero tenure \u2014 can never exceed level 3.\n\n**What I changed:**\n\n1. **`AssignOwnerMembershipLevel.java`** \u2014 reworked from a hard `MAX_LEVEL = 3` cap into a genuine additive rule where level 4 is a real, tenure-gated outcome:\n   - Base 1, `+1` email, `+1` unique name (`namesakeCount == 0`), and now `+1` when tenure exceeds `365` days, capped at `MAX_LEVEL = 4`.\n   - Since the top level is now the *only* one requiring the tenure step, level 4 genuinely \"requires tenure of more than 365 days\" \u2014 you can't reach it on the email/name factors alone (those max out at 3).\n   - Tenure is read from the **resolved registration-date variable** already published by `ResolveOwnerRegistrationDate` (reusing existing state rather than duplicating date logic), because in the POST pipeline `applyRegistrationDate` runs *after* this step, so the owner isn't stamped yet. Tenure = `ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(registrationDate, now)`, which is 0 (or negative) for a brand-new registration \u2192 the tenure step never fires at creation \u2192 new owners cap at 3.\n   - Kept the class small and single-purpose: extracted `hasEmail`, `hasUniqueName`, and `tenureDays` as focused private helpers.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 updated the `assignMembershipLevel` step comment to describe the new tenure factor and the level-3 cap for new owners.\n\nThe `coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3` acceptance test (email + unique name, zero tenure \u2192 expects level 3) passes, confirming a new owner lands at 3, not 4.",
